<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luis Sánchez Cerro</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1889">1889</a>–<a href="/years/1933">1933</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Luis Miguel Sánchez Cerro</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Luis Sánchez Cerro was a Peruvian army officer who overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augusto-b-leguia">Augusto B. Leguía</a> in August 1930 and, after a brief exile, won the disputed presidential election of 1931 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-raul-haya-de-la-torre">Víctor Raúl Haya de la Torre</a>. His government harshly repressed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-popular-revolutionary-alliance">APRA</a>, including after the Trujillo uprising of 1932, and went to war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> over Leticia. He was assassinated by a young APRA member in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> in April 1933.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luis-sanchez-cerro.jpg" alt="Luis Sánchez Cerro" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Toño Zapata (republished in 1999) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:S%C3%A1nchez_Cerro.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Luis Miguel Sánchez Cerro was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piura">Piura</a> on 12 August 1889, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mestizo">mestizo</a> descent. In 1914, during the overthrow of President Guillermo Billinghurst, he lost three fingers of his left hand when he seized the muzzle of a machine gun and turned it against government forces. In 1921 he was shot and injured when captured in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> in an unsuccessful attempt to overthrow President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augusto-b-leguia">Augusto B. Leguía</a>. In exile he served with the Spanish Foreign Legion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, held a post at the ministry of war in 1924, served with the Royal Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1925 and took advanced studies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1926. Leguía allowed him to return in 1928.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>On 22 August 1930, as a lieutenant-colonel, Sánchez Cerro overturned Leguía's eleven-year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> in a coup in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arequipa">Arequipa</a>. He was chosen president of the provisional junta on 27 August, but after six months navy officers told him that only one regiment in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> remained loyal, and he resigned and went into exile. He returned on 7 July 1931 to stand in the election of October, which he won by 19,745 votes as the candidate of the Revolutionary Union, which he founded; the opposition party <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-popular-revolutionary-alliance">APRA</a> contested the result.</p><p>As president he targeted perceived enemies in the military, the civil service, unions and the legislature, and his rule saw conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-popular-revolutionary-alliance">APRA</a> and a 1932 uprising in Trujillo. He survived a shooting attempt by José Melgar in March 1932, and in June 1932 a revolt in Huaraz led him to close the National College and the National University.</p><p>In September 1932 Peruvian civilians and soldiers dressed as civilians seized the Colombian town of Leticia. A treaty of 1922 had given <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> a corridor to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a>, but the new authorities considered it null. By February 1933 both countries had deployed troops along the Putumayo River, and the Council of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> told <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> to refrain from force on Colombian territory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 30 April 1933 Sánchez Cerro had just reviewed twenty thousand recruits at the Santa Beatriz racetrack in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> when Abelardo de Mendoza, a member of the suppressed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-popular-revolutionary-alliance">APRA</a>, shot him through the heart. Parliament chose General Óscar R. Benavides as provisional president.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Luis Miguel Sánchez Cerro” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piura">Piura</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-5.2,-80.63&amp;year=1917" aria-label="Piura on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2315 2358 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Luis Sánchez Cerro"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2765 2628h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2765 2628h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-5.2,-80.63&amp;year=1917">What was here in 1917? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
